@1 = ~Flying Ship of Halruaa~
@2 = ~I need to search the forest where Shandalar's ship first crashed. A silver control rod essential to restoring the flying ship is believed to be somewhere among the wreckage.~
@3 = ~I need to return to Shandalar and tell him that a gnome found the silver rod before us and fled with it.~
@4 = ~I need to travel to the Friendly Arm Inn and recover Shandalar's silver rod from Ervin Quill.~
@5 = ~I need to return to Shandalar and report what happened at the Friendly Arm Inn.~
@6 = ~I need to find Shandalar's daughters and tell them to return to their father.~
@7 = ~I have spoken to Shandalar's daughters. I need to return to Shandalar.~

@8 = ~I have been sent to the Ice Island as punishment. I need to find a way to survive and escape.~
@9 = ~I need to follow Delorna deeper into the caves and help her confront Shandalar.~
@10 = ~I need to decide when I am ready to leave the Ice Island with Delorna.~
@11 = ~I defeated Shandalar, broke his spell over the Ice Island, and escaped with Delorna.~
@12 = ~Y... you?~
@13 = ~You're still alive?~
@14 = ~Hah. I thought I killed you. This time I'll ensure I do!~
@15 = ~I managed to save myself. Can't say the same for Andris but he deserved it at least. Look, I apologise for what we tried to do but Andris had a short fuse. Angering him is a first step to a grave. Ever since Shandalar found a way out, we all wanted to do the same, so we followed the same path...~
@16 = ~We removed our equipment, hoping it would do the trick. Needless to say, it didn't. Andris decided to keep entertaining his initial idea, and gather as many unfortunate incoming victims as possible. He thought together, we can cause an explosion on a massive scale that could reshape the invulnerable barrier he believes encircles the island.~
@17 = ~His research led him to believe that collecting magical items from new travelers can result in the same outcome, and indulged that fashion instead. He started killing newcomers, taking their stuff and trying to manipulate the magic from their items to elevate his own powers. I never trusted him, but making alliances in a place like this keep you alive, and I wasn't waiting for him to kill me in my sleep, so I agreed.~
@18 = ~How did you end up here?~
@19 = ~Who else did survive?~
@20 = ~I don't care about your story! You attacked me, and that was your last mistake!~
@21 = ~Me and my friends formed an adventurer's group. Killing monsters, saving damsels in distress, getting paid, that kind of thing. Being an novice at the whole spellcasting, I overestimated my abilities. With confidence I cast a spell to take us from Sembia to Ruathym island, trying to prove my superiority and aptitude.~
@22 = ~I ended up here... I don't know what became of my friends. I fear, the instability of this place might've made them perish or die. I am saddened by their loss but I was unable to do anything.~
@23 = ~What else do you want to know?~
@24 = ~How did you end up here?~
@25 = ~Who else did survive?~
@26 = ~I don't care about your story! You attacked me, and that was your last mistake!~
@27 = ~Some mages on the lower levels. If you want, you can meet them on the lower levels, though not all of them are friendly. Just watch out for Shandalar's daughters. They're back.~
@28 = ~Do you have anything else to ask?~
@29 = ~Do you know this is Shandalar's design?~
@30 = ~Do you know a way out?~
@31 = ~If I find a way out, I'll help you.~
@32 = ~I presumed so. Dezkiel was onto him, before you butchered him. Shandalar's daughters are here right now, overseeing his experiment while he is away.~
@33 = ~What way out? There is no way out! Need I remind you, we are subjects to a sadistic experiment?~
@34 = ~You asked for it when you angered our father! Prepare to die!~
@35 = ~More food! Pets! Destroy these fools and bring me their meat!~
@36 = ~More unwilling subjects of Shandalar's experiment? Or have you been sent to finish me off? No matter. I'll take pleasure in using your items to warm myself up.~
@37 = ~Wait!~
@38 = ~Get out of my way. You want to end up like your sisters?~
@39 = ~How do we leave! Speak, now!~
@40 = ~Why are you here?~
@41 = ~Hold it! I am not on their side. I was helping them for way too long. My father isn't a saint and his endgame serves no one but himself. I can get you out but I need your help first.~
@42 = ~My help?~
@43 = ~That won't save you. You're lying to me.~
@44 = ~My father. He betrayed Halruaa, our core values and most of all, he betrayed us - his daughters. My sisters couldn't see it. Couldn't see that we are mere instruments for our father's machinations.~
@45 = ~I know you had to kill them. Despite how much I want to claw your eyes out, you did it in self defense. I just want this to be over, and I need you to... to kill my father.~
@46 = ~I am your only chance of survival. You won't leave this island without me. Only I can perform a ritual that will allow us to leave this accursed island. You kill me, and you'll never leave. Is that what you want? To be trapped on a cone of ice?~
@47 = ~I... guess I have no choice. Tell us what we need to do.~
@48 = ~Meet me deeper in the caves. My father will be there soon. I called him to meet me under pretence that you managed to escape from the island. This will be a perfect opportunity to strike him down for good. Follow these tunnels, and come all the way south.~
@49 = ~I have the means to teleport you out but I need you to help me first.~
@50 = ~My father. He arrived at the island too and I was stupid enough to listen to him. He betrayed Halruaa, our core values and most of all, he betrayed us - his daughters. My sisters couldn't see it. Couldn't see that we are mere instruments for our father's machinations, even after he already killed one of our sisters.~
@51 = ~I know you had to kill them. Despite how much I want to claw your eyes out, you did it in self defense. I just want this to be over, and I need you to... to kill my father.~
@52 = ~Me and my sisters were instructed by our father to check on the status of the mages. We knew they are being trapped here all along, but we couldn't do anything. I... couldn't do anything. My father would kill me for it. He already killed one of our sisters - Phrielmara for questioning his judgment. I need your help.~
@53 = ~Thank you! I appreciate it. I promised to get you out of here, and I shall abide by it. I wouldn't have done this without your help. Are you ready to leave?~
@54 = ~Yes. Take us out.~
@55 = ~Not yet. We want to take a look around.~
@56 = ~Splendid. Once again, I am sorry for all this. Good luck now. We won't see each other again.~
@57 = ~Delorna! What is this! You said these wretches escaped!~
@58 = ~You and I, Shandalar. Only one side can be victorious.~
@59 = ~I shall fight you to the death. But first tell me, why connive all this?~
@60 = ~So be it. Studying your reactions would be more interesting but one cannot be too choosy. I hope you have something more than empty threats to back up your words.~
@61 = ~Why? As a member of Elders I had everything! Power, wealth, authority! One mistake cost me everything! I was raised under vigorous watch of my father who knew I am destined for greater things. All I did was to render myself prestigious, as per my desire. Had I foreseen the unsought outcome of me treachery, I never would've planted my lies.~
@62 = ~I will conclude the sale with Liia Jannath, and I will make you suffer for intruding in my affairs and ruining my business. Then I will make Halruaans pay for what they did to me. They'll get used to the cold atmosphere. I'd be more worried about getting mad.~
@64 = ~Looking for this?~
@65 = ~We've been sent to retrieve it.~
@66 = ~Give it back if you wish to live.~
@67 = ~Shandalar sent you, huh? He played his cards well. Warn him, that if he stops meddling in my affairs, I shall leave him be. Now, I must depart. Fare thee well.~
@69 = ~You?!~
@70 = ~Me! Hand over what you stole from the crash site, you little thief!~
@71 = ~Me. I am only here to talk.~
@72 = ~If you attempt to teleport away again, I assure you only your headless corpse will emerge on the other side of the portal.~
@73 = ~Stole it? That rod belongs to the sovereign nation of Halruaa, not to that vile, megalomaniacal viper Shandalar! His traitorous blueprints will fall through without this vital catalyst, and I intend to deliver it safely to the Council Elders myself.~
@74 = ~Perhaps you possess a modicum more civility than the rest of the cutthroats roaming this lawless coast. Very well, mercenary. Let us speak before blades are drawn.~
@75 = ~Yield! I am standing perfectly still! You speak with immense hostility for someone who clearly remains blind to the true nature of their employer.~
@76 = ~Listen closely to what I have to tell you. Shandalar is a master of deception, a two-faced bastard who wraps unwitting fools around his fingers without breaking a sweat. He has fed you a silver-spoon diet of grandiose lies, but did he ever dare reveal the core of his exile?~
@77 = ~No. He didn't.~
@78 = ~Speak your piece, gnome, and pray I find it convincing.~
@79 = ~Shandalar was once a highly revered High Elder. I was his close friend - advisor even. But power is a slow poison, and it eroded my friend until nothing remained but a hollow shell of boundless ambition.~
@80 = ~I was the first to uncover his most blasphemous secret: he was actively practicing forbidden Netherese chronomancy and dark, reality-warping abjuration. Such magic is strictly outlawed by the Council due to its volatile, corrupting influence on the Weave. Our proud Shandalar became a textbook specimen of that decay.~
@81 = ~When I brought his heresies before the High Council, they moved to strip him of his arcana and status. But Shandalar anticipated us. He committed the unthinkable as he murdered Elder Ignacius in cold blood, plundered the state vaults for skyship schematics, draft-enlisted his daughters, and fled aboard Ignacius's hijacked flying vessel with his daughters.~
@82 = ~Yet, the most grotesque revelation lay hidden within the journal he left behind during his frantic flight. We unearthed the truth regarding his Island experiment. It was never a natural anomaly; it was a calculated, artificial dead-magic snare of his own design. He discovered that remote, frozen rock near Icewind Dale and used it as a private laboratory.~
@83 = ~He snared spellcasters from across Faerûn, forcing them into that frozen labyrinth just so he could study their despair. He even teleported himself inside, posing as a helpless victim to monitor their psychological collapse firsthand. It was a sick, twisted game to test the thresholds of his warding magic.~
@84 = ~His strategy changed later. He intends to trigger a devastating proxy war. By providing Baldur's Gate with the blueprints to manufacture an airborne armada, he hopes to force a desperate Halruaa into launching a full-scale pre-emptive military strike against the Sword Coast.~
@85 = ~While Halruaa tries to crush the shipyards of Baldur's Gate, Shandalar plans to slip away entirely unnoticed in the global chaos, his old rivals eliminated and his tracks permanently erased. You are nothing but disposable kindling for his war, my friend.~
@86 = ~The path you choose now rests entirely on your shoulders, friend. What say you?~
@87 = ~I believe you. He should be stopped.~
@88 = ~I don't trust you. Or him. I'll make my decision when the time comes.~
@89 = ~I'll stick to Shandalar. Cooperation with him seems profitable at least.~
@90 = ~Wise choice. I will take the rod. Inform Shandalar I got away with the rod once again. Let him know that I possess Amulet of Magic Altering. He'll believe you thanks to this. Obviously, I cannot use my magic to teleport across the seas, but I will take a ship back to my nation and share Shandalar's location with our agents. Just ensure he doesn't run. Do what he says, for the time being.~
@91 = ~Then all I got left is trust that you won't disappoint me. I am keeping the rod, whether you like it or not. Go back to Shandalar, but please, consider this as you make your way there. I'm off.~
@92 = ~You're just as vile as he is. Run back to your master and do as he wishes. You're no better than him. I am keeping the rod, whether you like it or not.~
@93 = ~You're my father's follower, aren't you?~
@94 = ~Yes. You and your sisters are to go to him straight away.~
@95 = ~I understand. We shall leave as soon as we finish our business here.~

@96 = ~I see you found your way here. I told you you wouldn't miss it.~
@97 = ~Home? You live on a ship?~
@98 = ~What did you want to talk about?~
@99 = ~That island you sent us to... what was that place?~
@100 = ~How do flying ships work?~
@101 = ~We'll leave you now.~
@102 = ~Anything else?~
@103 = ~Home? You live on a ship?~
@104 = ~What did you want to talk about?~
@105 = ~That island you sent us to... what was that place?~
@106 = ~How do flying ships work?~
@107 = ~We'll leave you now.~
@108 = ~Not just any ship. This is a Halruaan flying ship. Well... it used to be a "flying" ship. Now it's nothing more than a mere wreck in need of repairs that I haven't been able to get to as of late.~
@109 = ~You're welcome to take a look around, as long as you don't touch anything that isn't yours.~
@110 = ~The majority of these fine specimens and liquids are essential to my plans to restore this ship to the majestic piece of machinery it once was.~
@111 = ~What else would you like to know?~
@112 = ~What did you want to talk about?~
@113 = ~That island you sent us to... what was that place?~
@114 = ~How do flying ships work?~
@115 = ~We'll leave you now.~
@116 = ~You've gotten my cloak back, so I decided to send you on one more mission.~
@117 = ~I'm sure you've heard about the stolen components for the flying ship. Is that right?~
@118 = ~Maybe.~
@119 = ~Why would we?~
@120 = ~Cut it out. I know you were responsible for killing Resar and passing my components to the Thieves' Guild in Baldur's Gate. There's no need to hide it now. Have no fear; it's all forgiven, since I bought them back anyway. Obtaining these components in the first place took me a lifetime, and I'm still missing one essential object—a control rod.~
@121 = ~My daughters are currently protecting three components required to fix this junk. I want get airborne again and for that I need said components to mend the damage.~
@122 = ~Unfortunately, when the ship crashed, some of its components were lost. I can rebuild it, provided I get them back. I've found the majority of the missing pieces, but the silver rod is still somewhere among the junk and trinkets I couldn't locate.~
@123 = ~North of here, there is a forest where I initially crashed. I imagine the missing piece is somewhere in that area. I'll mark the place on your map. Here.~
@124 = ~Anything else?~
@125 = ~Home? You live on a ship?~
@126 = ~That island you sent us to... what was that place?~
@127 = ~How do flying ships work?~
@128 = ~We'll leave you now.~
@129 = ~That, my dear wanderer, even I cannot answer. The Weave surrounding the island has been twisted and altered using mystical magic that not even Elminster himself could undo. It faultlessly catches and imprisons any mage who tries to teleport across the sea.~
@130 = ~For a while, I pondered the possibility that the island was nothing more than a magical anomaly. After all, such places can exist. However, as someone who was once a prisoner on that island myself, I spent a great deal of time studying the place. Obviously, not much can be deduced from a frozen tomb like that, but think about the way the corridors down there are constructed.~
@131 = ~The entire complex beneath the island was chiseled by human hands. The maze of hallways seems to have been designed for... something. The only question is, what was its original purpose? The only way I managed to defy the laws of the island was by removing the material goods and items I possessed. Magic triggers the anomaly, whereas relinquishing it prevents the trigger.~
@132 = ~During my stay, Andris and I struck a deal. We wanted to combine our powers and escape, but he was already losing his mind from endless cold and hunger. At the time, we had all formed rather uneasy alliances and excluded certain individuals. Andris and I shared quite a history, but we decided to join forces and attempt to find a way out together.~
@133 = ~Then there was Tellan—a demented gnomish mage who demanded that we share our secrets with him. He was even more mind-broken than Cuchol. Poor fool. Andris used his powers to frighten the idiot, but it wasn't enough. He started scheming and watching us more cautiously.~
@134 = ~Our plans reached fever pitch, however, when Andris and I came to a disagreement. He believed that combining the magical powers of every mage together could cause an explosion capable of reshaping the Weave and creating a rift of sorts. He thought this could be our way out. That's when I understood that he and I had gravely misunderstood each other.~
@135 = ~My notion was that the only way to escape was to reduce magical energy to a minimum. Logically, if spellcasting traps you, it is unwise to keep repeating the same pattern that got you imprisoned in the first place. Our interpretations were polar opposites, and Andris began threatening me.~
@136 = ~Some mages took his side. Garan isolated himself, thinking we'd all gone mad, and Dezkiel... he was silent. He never spoke much and kept to himself, and my suspicions grew. He was onto one of us, I thought. I hoped he trusted Andris more than me.~
@137 = ~Meanwhile, I abandoned my robes and scrolls and completely rejected my old ways. I forsook magic in exchange for freedom. It worked out perfectly. One night, I went to sleep, and I awoke to the sound of waves. I was lying on the beach near Murann. Inexplicably, I had managed to evade my fate. How the laws of that island work is beyond even my understanding.~
@138 = ~My cloak is an... object of immense power, which I need for my own reasons. Getting it back would have been... dangerous for a mage of my renown. But since I had developed a wardstone capable of safely bypassing the magnetic fields of the island, I could send an outsider to retrieve it.~
@139 = ~What else would you like to know?~
@140 = ~Do you regret leaving your comrades there?~
@141 = ~Did you ever kill anyone whilst on the island?~
@142 = ~How did you develop a wardstone?~
@143 = ~That's all I wanted to know. Thank you for sharing.~
@144 = ~Comrades? They were nothing more than imbeciles. Tellan believed he was a shining light of Tethyr. Cuchol was a philanderer who committed vile acts against women, including a noblewoman from Surkh called Yspank. Of them all, he deserved this fate the most, in my opinion. Marcellus was a mere merchant from Waterdeep who dabbled in magic as a hobby. Beyn was a member of an adventuring group from Sembia, and Garan was a bestiary keeper at Neverwinter Academy.~
@145 = ~Ever since they became trapped on the island, it warped their minds. Their desire to escape slowly began erasing their humanity, piece by piece. Aside from Beyn, I cannot say I regret their deaths at your hands. After all, they have left that cursed place. Not in the way they would have preferred, I imagine, but at least they are free from the cold.~
@146 = ~What else would you like to know?~
@147 = ~Did you ever kill anyone whilst on the island?~
@148 = ~How did you develop a wardstone?~
@149 = ~That's all I wanted to know. Thank you for sharing.~
@150 = ~Personally, no. Beyn, Marcellus, and I never meant any harm to those trapped with us. On the contrary, Andris hoped for more victims, as this would further his belief that we could produce a gargantuan explosion. The other mages had no care in the world and chose transgression and hostility instead.~
@151 = ~Of course, new mages are constantly drawn to the island, so I cannot speak for those I never met. And frankly, I have no need to dwell on it anymore.~
@152 = ~What else would you like to know?~
@153 = ~Do you regret leaving your comrades there?~
@154 = ~How did you develop a wardstone?~
@155 = ~That's all I wanted to know. Thank you for sharing.~
@156 = ~Halruaans have secrets, my friend. I would have to kill you had I shared them.~
@157 = ~What else would you like to know?~
@158 = ~Do you regret leaving your comrades there?~
@159 = ~Did you ever kill anyone whilst on the island?~
@160 = ~That's all I wanted to know. Thank you for sharing.~
@161 = ~Amazing question. I'm glad you asked. In order to make a ship fly, we use the shells of sea turtles found in Halruaa. They're affixed to the underside of the ship and imbued with a special kind of magic.~
@162 = ~The under-hull plates connect to a central control rod made of silver and capped with gold. By sliding golden sleeves marked with runes along the silver rod, the helmsman controls the ship's levitation, pitch, and vertical ascent or descent.~
@163 = ~Once aloft, the ship relies on its square sails and steering panels to catch the wind and move laterally, much like a regular boat on water. They can also land and float safely on actual water. The magical apparatus requires continuous recharging by specialised wizards once a year, sometimes more often.~
@164 = ~I hope I've satisfied your curiosity.~
@165 = ~What else would you like to know?~
@166 = ~Home? You live on a ship?~
@167 = ~What did you want to talk about?~
@168 = ~That island you sent us to... what was that place?~
@169 = ~We'll leave you now.~
@170 = ~You know the way back, I trust.~
@171 = ~Oh, you're my father's colleagues, huh? You don't look impressive enough in my eyes, but each to their own.~
@172 = ~Leave me be. I want some peace.~

// Batch 27: Shandalar continuation, including daughters-skip text.
@173 = ~So? Did you find the silver rod?~
@174 = ~We searched the wreckage for the silver rod as you requested, but a gnome found it before us and fled.~
@175 = ~A gnome? Damn! It must have been Ervin Quill. That miserable, opportunistic little wretch... We were colleagues once, until his petty jealousy cost me my seat on the Council of Elders.~
@176 = ~You were an Elder of Halruaa?~
@177 = ~Indeed. The Council of Elders is the high magocratic ruling body of Halruaa—the absolute apex of arcane prestige. I spent decades as one of its most influential voices, shaping the destiny of the south.~
@178 = ~Ervin was my advisor, a man I foolishly deemed loyal until the rot of ambition consumed him. Our rivalry eventually backed me into a corner, triggering a cascade of catastrophic events that ended with my unjust exile. Naturally, I did not leave empty-handed; I commandeered a skyship and fled.~
@179 = ~My daughters and I intended to seek asylum here on the Sword Coast, but the vessel suffered a severe magical malfunction and plunged into the northern canopy. I managed to coax the wounded craft into the air just long enough to ground it safely near Ulgoth's Beard, but the hull was ruined. I had to pivot, and quickly.~
@180 = ~My enemies back home dispatched assassins the moment they realized what I was planning. My own sentimentality proved my undoing; in our frantic escape, I left behind my personal journal. When the Council's agents ransacked my abandoned estate, they uncovered the ledger and parsed my grand design.~
@181 = ~You see, my true endgame was to offer my salvaged home to the highest bidder. Prior to my departure, I secured the core components necessary to replicate Halruaan skyship technology. I intended to trade these secrets directly to Grand Duke Liia Jannath.~
@182 = ~You rather clumsily disrupted that arrangement by thieving the components from Oberan's Estate. Yet, I am a wizard of variable metrics. While you were busy digging through forest detritus for my silver rod, I used my local influence to buy the components back from the Thieves' Guild. Business, as they say, marches on.~
@183 = ~I shall summon my daughters shortly to retrieve the relics so I may conclude my audience with the Duchess. The preliminary negotiations are already settled, and her interest is absolute.~
@184 = ~Hunted by state-sanctioned killers, I require absolute geopolitical leverage. By granting Baldur's Gate a total monopoly on aerial warfare and trade along the Sword Coast, I buy myself permanent immunity, infinite political sanctuary, and massive institutional power. I will be entirely untouchable.~
@185 = ~To house the artifacts safely, I commandeered the third floor of Oberan's Estate. He is a high-society simpleton, so thoroughly blinded by his loyalty to Liia Jannath that he happily surrendered his mansion to my daughters, believing he was performing a classified, patriotic service for the Grand Duke. A perfect arrangement.~
@186 = ~Yet, the Council's reach is long. They sent Resar—a high elder and once a dear friend of mine—to enforce their draconian laws. Halruaa treats the proliferation of skyship blueprints as a treasonous capital offense. The penalty is total: the stripping of all wealth, the absolute severing of one's magic, and swift execution. I walked into that fire, but I do not intend to burn.~
@187 = ~What would you like to know?~
@188 = ~Why did the Council banish you in the first place?~
@189 = ~What is our next move?~
@190 = ~That is all very fascinating, but how do we get the rod back?~
@191 = ~I have no intention of sharing my private histories with mercenary wanderers. Suffice it to say, my genius outpaced their small, isolationist minds.~
@192 = ~First, work on your patience. Our paramount objective is to apprehend Ervin Quill. I shall weave a quick divination to locate the little thief... shouldn't be too hard if he's still in the area.~
@193 = ~Ah... got him. He is currently downing ale within the safety of the Friendly Arm Inn, wholly oblivious to the storm brewing over his head. Travel there, reclaim my silver rod, and brook no delays. But heed my warning: Ervin is a silver-tongued schemer. Do not trust a single word he utters. I shall await your return.~
@194 = ~Ach, you're back. You have my rod?~
@195 = ~Things have changed. My loyalties have changed.~
@196 = ~No. Ervin fled again. He had the Amulet of Magic Altering.~
@197 = ~No. He fled again.~
@198 = ~Not yet. I'll be back soon though.~
@199 = ~Changed? You aren't thinking of double-crossing me, are you?~
@200 = ~Drop the charade. Ervin told us everything.~
@201 = ~Fair enough. My plan was to throw you back on the Ice Island and watch you beg for mercy as I am lavishing in Baldur's Gate, waiting to corrupt the system. But since you're asking so nicely, I'll sent you back now.~
@202 = ~Blasted fool. I should've known he took it. No matter, he'll be found in time. As for you, my little pet, I have another task for you.~
@203 = ~No matter. He'll be caught one way or the other. As for you, my little pet, I have another task for you.~
@204 = ~Speak to my daughters and tell them to return to me at once. Other Halruaans cannot be far behind, and I need to move.~
@205 = ~You return without my daughters? No matter. I have no further use for that errand—or for you.~
@206 = ~There is no need to send you to Oberan's Estate. That business is settled, and you have outlived your usefulness. I tracked down Ervin and dealt with him myself. As punishment for your incompetence, you shall rot on the Ice Island. You would have ended up there even if you had remained on my side.~
@207 = ~I see you return. My daughters are already waiting for you, and so will I be. You've outlived your usefulness. I managed to track down Ervin and behead him myself, so you weren't vital for my goals. As a punishment for your incompetence, you shall rot on the Ice Island. I'm sure you already know it's my creation. Feel no disappointment over your action. You would've been sent there even if you remained on my side.~
